00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,300
Set Phrases, a Cultural Indoctrination to American Norms When I learned the alphabet

2
00:00:06,300 --> 00:00:11,940
as a child, I heard it before I saw it. I heard that the last four letters were W,

3
00:00:11,980 --> 00:00:17,820
X, Y, Z. I thought that W was a long, strange name for a letter, but I didn't

4
00:00:17,820 --> 00:00:23,760
question it any more than I did H. It was just a name. Many years later, it struck

5
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:31,420
me that it was a double U. Of course, a W is really UU. Funny feeling, though, when

6
00:00:31,420 --> 00:00:36,340
I realized that something I had taken for granted for so many years had a background

7
00:00:36,340 --> 00:00:41,420
meaning that I had completely overlooked. This funny feeling is exactly what most

8
00:00:41,420 --> 00:00:47,180
native speakers get when a two-word phrase is stressed on the wrong word. When two

9
00:00:47,180 --> 00:00:51,900
individual words go through the cultural process of becoming a set phrase, the

10
00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:56,020
original sense of each word is more or less forgotten and the new meaning

11
00:00:56,020 --> 00:01:02,660
completely takes over. When we hear the word painkiller, we think anesthetic. If,

12
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:07,260
however, someone says painkiller, it brings up the strength and almost

13
00:01:07,260 --> 00:01:13,080
unrelated meaning of kill. When you have a two-word phrase, you have to put stress on

14
00:01:13,080 --> 00:01:18,340
either the first word or the second word. If you stress both or neither, it's not

15
00:01:18,340 --> 00:01:23,560
clear what you are trying to say. Stress on the first word is more noticeable and

16
00:01:23,560 --> 00:01:29,220
one of the most important concepts of that you are going to study. At first glance,

17
00:01:29,420 --> 00:01:34,220
it doesn't seem significant, but the more you look at this concept, the more you are

18
00:01:34,220 --> 00:01:38,840
going to realize that it reflects how we Americans think, what concepts we have

19
00:01:38,840 --> 00:01:44,940
adopted as our own, and what things we consider important. Set phrases are our

20
00:01:44,940 --> 00:01:49,580
cultural icons or word images. They are indicators of a determined use that we

21
00:01:49,580 --> 00:01:54,300
have internalized. These set phrases, with stress on the first word, have been taken

22
00:01:54,300 --> 00:01:58,640
into everyday English from descriptive phrases with stress on the second word. As

23
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:02,560
soon as a descriptive phrase becomes a set phrase, the emphasis shifts from the

24
00:02:02,560 --> 00:02:06,340
second word to the first. The original sense of each word is more or less

25
00:02:06,340 --> 00:02:10,580
forgotten and the new meaning takes over. Set phrases indicate that we have

26
00:02:10,580 --> 00:02:14,900
internalized this phrase as an image, that we all agree on a concrete idea that this

27
00:02:14,900 --> 00:02:19,740
phrase represents. A hundred years or so ago, when Levi Strauss first came out with

28
00:02:19,740 --> 00:02:24,360
his denim pants, they were described as blue jeans. We all agree on the image,

29
00:02:24,400 --> 00:02:28,520
however, they are blue jeans. A more recent example would be the descriptive

30
00:02:28,520 --> 00:02:33,120
phrase, he's a real party animal. This slang expression refers to someone who has

31
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:37,320
a great time at a party. When it first became popular, the people using it needed

32
00:02:37,320 --> 00:02:42,000
to explain with their intonation that he was an animal at a party. As time passed,

33
00:02:42,040 --> 00:02:45,940
the expression became cliche and we changed the intonation to, he's a real

34
00:02:45,940 --> 00:02:50,940
party animal because everyone knew what it meant. Clichés are hard to recognize in a

35
00:02:50,940 --> 00:02:54,800
new language because what may be an old and tired expression to a native speaker

36
00:02:54,800 --> 00:02:59,260
may be fresh and exciting to a newcomer. One way to look at English from the inside

37
00:02:59,260 --> 00:03:03,400
out, rather than always looking from the outside in, is to get a feel for what

38
00:03:03,400 --> 00:03:07,580
Americans have already accepted and internalized. This starts out as a purely

39
00:03:07,580 --> 00:03:11,480
language phenomenon, but you will notice that as you progress and undergo the

40
00:03:11,480 --> 00:03:15,160
relentless cultural indoctrination of standard intonation patterns, you will

41
00:03:15,160 --> 00:03:18,800
find yourself expressing yourself with the language cues and signals that will mark

42
00:03:18,800 --> 00:03:23,700
you as an insider, not an outsider. When the interpreter was translating for the

43
00:03:23,700 --> 00:03:28,120
former Russian president Gorbachev about his trip to San Francisco in 1990, his

44
00:03:28,120 --> 00:03:31,740
pronunciation was good, but he placed himself on the outside by repeatedly

45
00:03:31,740 --> 00:03:37,740
saying, cable car. The phrase cable car is an image, an established entity, and was

46
00:03:37,740 --> 00:03:42,000
very noticeable to hear it stressed on the second word as a mere description. An

47
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:45,720
important point that I would like to make is that the rules you are given here are

48
00:03:45,720 --> 00:03:49,780
not meant to be memorized. This discussion is only an introduction to give you a

49
00:03:49,780 --> 00:03:54,660
starting point in understanding and in recognizing what to listen for. Read it

50
00:03:54,660 --> 00:03:59,700
over, think about it, then listen, try it out, listen some more, and try it out

51
00:03:59,700 --> 00:04:03,780
again. As you become familiar with intonation, you will become more

52
00:04:03,780 --> 00:04:08,260
comfortable with American norms, thus the cultural orientation or even cultural

53
00:04:08,260 --> 00:04:13,000
indoctrination aspect of the following examples. Note, when you get the

54
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:16,560
impression that a two-word description could be hyphenated or even made into one

55
00:04:16,560 --> 00:04:21,540
word, it's a signal that it could be a set phrase. For example, flashlight,

56
00:04:22,200 --> 00:04:28,180
flash, light, flash, light. Also, stress the first word with street, main street,

57
00:04:28,760 --> 00:04:33,140
and nationalities of food and people. Mexican food, Chinese girls.
